<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#insulin">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Insulin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1921–1922</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Insulin is the hormone that regulates blood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, and its absence or failure causes diabetes. It was isolated at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> in 1921–1922 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-banting">Frederick Banting</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-best">Charles Best</a>, with J. J. R. Macleod and James Collip, and first given to a patient in January 1922. The discovery made diabetes treatable and earned Banting and Macleod the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in 1923.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/insulin.jpg" alt="Insulin" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:AtikaAtikawa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Insulin_struct.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
